{:edb/db-before-t 2083 :edb/db-after-t 2084 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535396 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:17:16.385Z" 13194139535396 true] [17592186134214 1100 "$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}6x\\,dy]dA,\n$$\n\nEvaluate the integral above over the right bounded region $D$ in the $xz$-plane: $0\\le x\\le2$ and $0\\le z\\le4-x^2$. Its boundary consists of $z=0$, $x=0$, and $z+x^2=4$, on the nonnegative-$x$ side." 13194139535396 true] [17592186134214 1100 "$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}6x\\,dy]dA,\n$$\n\nEvaluate the repeated integral above given that $D$ is a region in the $xz$-plane bounded by the lines $z=0,$ $x=0,$ and the curve $z+{x}^{2}=4.$" 13194139535396 false] [17592186134214 1145 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}6x\\,dy]dA & ={∬}_{D}6x[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=4}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6x(4-{x}^{2})\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24x-6{x}^{3}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate this double integral, we plot $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/assets/612c4fda39f7003024c322b6d1fa5c279815923905c8d023547a3be6b4a6805a.png)\n\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $z$ and then with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}24x-6{x}^{3}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}24x-6{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(24x-6{x}^{3})[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(24x-6{x}^{3})(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}96x-48{x}^{3}+6{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[48{x}^{2}-12{x}^{4}+{x}^{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(192-192+64)-0 \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535396 false] [17592186134214 1145 "The clarified region is the right bounded lobe, $D=\\{(x,z):0\\le x\\le2,0\\le z\\le4-x^2\\}$. Integrate first with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\\int_{x^2}^4 6x\\,dy=6x(4-x^2).$$\n\nThe $z$ interval has length $4-x^2$, so\n\n$$\\iint_D\\left[\\int_{x^2}^4 6x\\,dy\\right]dA=\\int_0^2 6x(4-x^2)^2\\,dx=[-(4-x^2)^3]_0^2=64.$$\n\nAll inner limits are ordered. The original boundary description also encloses a left lobe, $-2\\le x\\le0$, whose integral is $-64$; it did not explicitly select a side of $x=0$. Selecting the right lobe explicitly gives $64$." 13194139535396 true]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
